      "hints": [
        "Set $f'(x) < 0 \\implies 3(2x - 1)^{1/2} < 6 \\implies (2x - 1)^{1/2} < 2$.",
        "Square to get $2x - 1 < 4 \\implies x < \\frac{5}{2}$, and combine with domain $x > \\frac{1}{2}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "For $f$ to be decreasing, the first derivative must be strictly negative:\n$$f'(x) < 0 \\implies 3(2x - 1)^{1/2} - 6 < 0$$",
        "Divide by $3$ and isolate the square root:\n$$(2x - 1)^{1/2} < 2$$",
        "Square both sides (valid since the square root is non-negative on $x > \\frac{1}{2}$):\n$$2x - 1 < 4 \\implies 2x < 5 \\implies x < \\frac{5}{2}$$",
        "Combine with the function's domain constraint $x > \\frac{1}{2}$:\n$$\\frac{1}{2} < x < \\frac{5}{2} \\quad \\left(\\text{or } \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\right)$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Decreasing Functions": "Sets $f'(x) < 0$ (M1). Solves $2x - 1 < 4$ (A1). States $\\frac{1}{2} < x < \\frac{5}{2}$ (A2)."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
