      "hints": [
        "Integrate $f'(x) = 3(2x - 1)^{1/2} - 6$ to get $f(x) = (2x - 1)^{3/2} - 6x + c$.",
        "Substitute $f(1) = -3$ to find $c = 2$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Integrate the derivative with respect to $x$ using the reverse chain rule:\n$$f(x) = \\int \\left(3(2x - 1)^{1/2} - 6\\right) dx = 3 \\times \\frac{(2x - 1)^{3/2}}{\\frac{3}{2} \\times 2} - 6x + c = (2x - 1)^{3/2} - 6x + c$$",
        "Substitute the known boundary condition $f(1) = -3$:\n$$-3 = (2(1) - 1)^{3/2} - 6(1) + c = 1^{3/2} - 6 + c = 1 - 6 + c = -5 + c$$\n$$c = -3 + 5 = 2$$",
        "State the complete function $f(x)$:\n$$f(x) = (2x - 1)^{3/2} - 6x + 2$$"
      ],
